BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dc312f28a6337739@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260908T210822Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine Heads to The Big E with Food\, Farms\, 
 and Makers
DESCRIPTION:Home\nSkip to top navigation\nSkip to side navigation\nSkip to
  content\nSkip to footer\nMaine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp
 \n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nDACF Home\n→\nAbout DACF\n→\nNewsroom\n→ News
 \nMaine Heads to The Big E with Food\, Farms\, and Makers\nSeptember 3\, 2
 026\nThe Maine Building at the Big E returns for 17 days of Maine food\, f
 arms\, makers\, and traditions\, September 18 - October 4\nAUGUSTA\, Maine
  (September 3\, 2026) - From loaded Maine baked potatoes and lobster rolls
  to wild blueberry crisp\, maple milkshakes\, smoked salmon\, and wood-fir
 ed pizza featuring Maine ingredients\, the tastes and traditions of Maine 
 are returning to\nThe Big E\nthis fall.\nThe State of Maine Building\, loc
 ated on the\nAvenue of States\nalongside buildings representing the five o
 ther New England states\, will welcome visitors throughout the 17-day East
 ern States Exposition\, September 18 through October 4\, 2026\, in West Sp
 ringfield\, Massachusetts. Maine Day will be celebrated on Saturday\, Sept
 ember 19.\nFor more than a century\, the Maine Building has introduced gen
 erations of fairgoers to the people\, products\, and traditions that make 
 Maine stand apart. The Maine Department of Agriculture\, Conservation and 
 Forestry (DACF) owns and operates the building as a showcase for Maine agr
 iculture\, food\, fisheries\, forests\, tourism\, artisans\, and businesse
 s.\n"The Big E gives Maine a special opportunity to showcase our food\, fa
 rms\, and agricultural businesses to people from across New England and be
 yond\,"\nsaid DACF Commissioner Amanda Beal\n. "Food is one of the most im
 mediate and memorable ways to make that connection. Someone may come into 
 the Maine Building for a baked potato\, lobster roll\, or a maple or wild 
 blueberry dish\, but they leave with a greater appreciation for the farmer
 s\, producers\, and small businesses behind those products."\nThe Big E is
  one of the largest fairs in North America\, providing Maine businesses wi
 th access to an enormous regional marketplace. For exhibiting producers an
 d makers\, the opp\nhttps://www.maine.gov/dacf/about/news/news.shtml?id=13
 362241
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dacf/about/news/news.shtml?id=13362241
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
